{
  "name": "happy",
  "integration": "netdisco",
  "description": "The full shape of a Netdisco collection: token login, one page of devices, per-device IP aliases, per-device located nodes, and the IP Inventory report joining node MACs to addresses. Row shapes come from the DBIC result classes that define the tables the API serialises - lib/App/Netdisco/DB/Result/Device.pm, DeviceIp.pm, Node.pm - and from the IpInventory report's own select list (ip, mac, dns, time_last, time_first, active, node, age, vendor, nbname). Proves: the api_key from POST /login is carried as a PLAIN Authorization header rather than a Bearer token; a device merges its management IP with its device_ip aliases; a node seen by two switches produces ONE asset whose primary location is the more recent sighting; the IP Inventory report attaches an address and a DNS name to a node that had only a MAC; and Netdisco's zone-less local timestamps are parsed without aborting. The age filter is disabled here (max_age_days=0) so this scenario cannot rot: an assertion that a node is 'too old' would depend on how long ago the fixture was written. The age filter has its own scenario, node-age. no_mac_in_id is skipped for every netdisco scenario: a located node has no identifier but its MAC, and the id is deliberately paired with no-id-match no-id-break so it never drives a merge.",
